1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,400
Which route should this packet take? The path
with fewer links

2
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:10,800
looks promising, but the costs tell a
different story. Let's follow

3
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:16,200
how a router can discover a better answer,
then adapt when

4
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:21,600
that answer breaks.

5
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:26,792
Represent the network as a graph: points are
devices, lines are

6
00:00:26,792 --> 00:00:31,983
connections, and numbers are costs. These
positive example costs aren't automatically

7
00:00:31,983 --> 00:00:37,175
seconds or distance. They define what cheaper
means in the problem

8
00:00:37,175 --> 00:00:42,367
we're solving.

9
00:00:42,367 --> 00:00:47,658
A-B-D-F has three links and costs fifteen.
A-C-B-D-E-F has five links

10
00:00:47,658 --> 00:00:52,950
and costs thirteen. More steps, lower total.
The drawing's apparent shortcut

11
00:00:52,950 --> 00:00:58,242
loses as soon as we add the numbers along both
complete

12
00:00:58,242 --> 00:01:03,533
paths.

13
00:01:03,533 --> 00:01:08,825
Before calculating, separate routing from
forwarding. Routing builds the choices;
forwarding

14
00:01:08,825 --> 00:01:14,117
uses them to move a packet to its next hop. A

15
00:01:14,117 --> 00:01:19,408
packet can follow an established choice
without running a new shortest-path

16
00:01:19,408 --> 00:01:24,700
calculation at every arrival.

17
00:01:24,700 --> 00:01:29,892
Start Dijkstra's algorithm at A. Its distance
is zero; others begin

18
00:01:29,892 --> 00:01:35,083
at infinity, meaning no route has been found.
Repeatedly settle the

19
00:01:35,083 --> 00:01:40,275
lowest unfinished estimate, then ask whether
routes through it improve the

20
00:01:40,275 --> 00:01:45,467
remaining estimates.

21
00:01:45,467 --> 00:01:50,650
From A, the first candidates are B at four and
C

22
00:01:50,650 --> 00:01:55,833
at two. These are tentative distances.
Reaching a node once doesn't

23
00:01:55,833 --> 00:02:01,017
prove we found its cheapest route; another
connection might still offer

24
00:02:01,017 --> 00:02:06,200
a lower total.

25
00:02:06,200 --> 00:02:11,492
C has the smallest estimate, so settle it
next. Through C,

26
00:02:11,492 --> 00:02:16,783
B now costs two plus one: three instead of
four. D

27
00:02:16,783 --> 00:02:22,075
becomes ten and E twelve. One settled point
has improved several

28
00:02:22,075 --> 00:02:27,367
unfinished possibilities.

29
00:02:27,367 --> 00:02:32,558
Next comes B at three. Its link to D costs
five,

30
00:02:32,558 --> 00:02:37,750
giving a total of eight. Replace D's old ten
and remember

31
00:02:37,750 --> 00:02:42,942
B as its predecessor. The number improves, and
we save the

32
00:02:42,942 --> 00:02:48,133
reason it improved.

33
00:02:48,133 --> 00:02:53,425
D is now cheapest at eight. Exploring its
connections improves E

34
00:02:53,425 --> 00:02:58,717
to ten and discovers F at fourteen. We have
reached the

35
00:02:58,717 --> 00:03:04,008
destination in our search, but shouldn't stop
yet: a cheaper unfinished

36
00:03:04,008 --> 00:03:09,300
route may still exist.

37
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:14,592
Settle E at ten and add its three-cost link to
F.

38
00:03:14,592 --> 00:03:19,883
Thirteen beats fourteen. Update F and record E
as its predecessor.

39
00:03:19,883 --> 00:03:25,175
The earlier destination estimate was useful,
but treating it as final

40
00:03:25,175 --> 00:03:30,467
would have missed this improvement.

41
00:03:30,467 --> 00:03:35,767
F's thirteen is now the smallest unfinished
distance, so settle it.

42
00:03:35,767 --> 00:03:41,067
The final distances from A are zero, three,
two, eight, ten,

43
00:03:41,067 --> 00:03:46,367
and thirteen. With nonnegative link costs, the
algorithm's settling rule makes

44
00:03:46,367 --> 00:03:51,667
those choices safe.

45
00:03:51,667 --> 00:03:56,958
The costs alone aren't the route. Follow
predecessors backward from F:

46
00:03:56,958 --> 00:04:02,250
E, D, B, C, A. Reverse that chain to recover
the

47
00:04:02,250 --> 00:04:07,542
forward path. Each saved improvement left one
clue that now reconstructs

48
00:04:07,542 --> 00:04:12,833
the entire journey.

49
00:04:12,833 --> 00:04:18,233
For packets leaving A toward F, the next hop
is C.

50
00:04:18,233 --> 00:04:23,633
The table can store that immediate choice
rather than a full

51
00:04:23,633 --> 00:04:29,033
itinerary inside each packet. In real
networks, entries commonly refer to

52
00:04:29,033 --> 00:04:34,433
destination address prefixes.

53
00:04:34,433 --> 00:04:39,625
Distance-vector routing starts with different
information. A neighbor advertises its
distance

54
00:04:39,625 --> 00:04:44,817
to a destination; we add the cost of reaching
that neighbor.

55
00:04:44,817 --> 00:04:50,008
Compare those totals, and select the best
current option through the

56
00:04:50,008 --> 00:04:55,200
neighbors we know.

57
00:04:55,200 --> 00:05:00,275
B advertises ten to F; reaching B costs four,
making fourteen.

58
00:05:00,275 --> 00:05:05,350
C advertises eleven; reaching C costs two,
making thirteen. C wins.

59
00:05:05,350 --> 00:05:10,425
The smallest advertised distance alone wasn't
enough; the local link cost

60
00:05:10,425 --> 00:05:15,500
matters too.

61
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:21,017
Link-state routing shares local connection
facts instead. Routers distribute which links

62
00:05:21,017 --> 00:05:26,533
exist and their costs, build a map, and
calculate routes from

63
00:05:26,533 --> 00:05:32,050
it. The shared information describes the
network's pieces, rather than only

64
00:05:32,050 --> 00:05:37,567
destination-distance estimates.

65
00:05:37,567 --> 00:05:44,911
Both approaches seek useful routes, but
exchange different evidence: destination
estimates

66
00:05:44,911 --> 00:05:52,256
or link facts. Neither learns changes
instantly. Messages must travel, and

67
00:05:52,256 --> 00:05:59,600
routers need time to revise what they believe
about the network.

68
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:04,892
Remove the D-to-E link. Our thirteen-cost
route is broken. A-C-B-D-F now

69
00:06:04,892 --> 00:06:10,183
costs fourteen, beating the fifteen-cost
alternative through C and E. A

70
00:06:10,183 --> 00:06:15,475
route is optimal only for the connections and
costs that still

71
00:06:15,475 --> 00:06:20,767
exist.

72
00:06:20,767 --> 00:06:26,058
That change doesn't reach every router at
once. Failure detection, update

73
00:06:26,058 --> 00:06:31,350
delivery, and recalculation take time. During
convergence, routers can briefly hold

74
00:06:31,350 --> 00:06:36,642
inconsistent views. The network is updating
many local decisions, not replacing

75
00:06:36,642 --> 00:06:41,933
one central map instantly.

76
00:06:41,933 --> 00:06:47,342
Watch two neighbors trust stale advice from
each other. Each believes

77
00:06:47,342 --> 00:06:52,750
the other still reaches the destination, so
their estimates keep increasing.

78
00:06:52,750 --> 00:06:58,158
This is counting to infinity. In RIP, sixteen
represents unreachable, limiting

79
00:06:58,158 --> 00:07:03,567
that particular count.

80
00:07:03,567 --> 00:07:09,075
Poisoned reverse blocks a specific circular
claim. If A reaches a

81
00:07:09,075 --> 00:07:14,583
destination through C, it tells C that the
destination is unreachable

82
00:07:14,583 --> 00:07:20,092
through A. That helps prevent two-node loops,
though it doesn't eliminate

83
00:07:20,092 --> 00:07:25,600
every possible routing loop.

84
00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:30,892
Change the costs and a different path can win.
Shortest describes

85
00:07:30,892 --> 00:07:36,183
the selected metric, not necessarily physical
distance or fewest hops. Real

86
00:07:36,183 --> 00:07:41,475
choices can also depend on policy, so
understanding the objective matters

87
00:07:41,475 --> 00:07:46,767
before comparing routes.

88
00:07:46,767 --> 00:07:52,175
At larger scales, sharing every detail
everywhere becomes expensive. Hierarchy groups

89
00:07:52,175 --> 00:07:57,583
networks and summarizes address blocks,
reducing the information each part must

90
00:07:57,583 --> 00:08:02,992
handle. The trade-off is deciding which
details remain local and which

91
00:08:02,992 --> 00:08:08,400
need wider visibility.

92
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:13,800
The opening packet chose a five-link route
costing thirteen, then a

93
00:08:13,800 --> 00:08:19,200
different route costing fourteen after
failure. The result wasn't a clever-looking

94
00:08:19,200 --> 00:08:24,600
line on a map. It came from comparing costs
and keeping

95
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:30,000
distributed information current.
